{"id":58,"course_id":18,"title":"HTTP, TLS, and Ingress","summary":"Read request symptoms across L7 boundaries.","sequence":3,"body_simplified":"## Mental model\nIngress, ALB, Nginx, app servers, and clients all speak in status codes, headers, certificates, and timeouts. You need to locate which hop generated the symptom.\n\n## What to practice\nPractice curl-style checks for status, headers, SNI, redirects, and health endpoints.\n\n## Practice checkpoint\nClassify 404, 502, 503, TLS handshake failure, and timeout into likely ownership areas.\n\n## Operator habit\nWrite down the symptom, the evidence, the suspected owner, and the safest next command before changing any live object. This turns scattered platform knowledge into a repeatable engineering workflow.","body_traditional":"## Mental model\nIngress, ALB, Nginx, app servers, and clients all speak in status codes, headers, certificates, and timeouts. You need to locate which hop generated the symptom.\n\n## What to practice\nPractice curl-style checks for status, headers, SNI, redirects, and health endpoints.\n\n## Practice checkpoint\nClassify 404, 502, 503, TLS handshake failure, and timeout into likely ownership areas.\n\n## Operator habit\nWrite down the symptom, the evidence, the suspected owner, and the safest next command before changing any live object. This turns scattered platform knowledge into a repeatable engineering workflow.","pinyin":"Scenario: You are the platform engineer on call for a realistic team environment.\nChecklist:\n- State the user impact in one sentence.\n- Identify the system boundary and owner.\n- Collect read-only evidence first.\n- Propose the smallest reversible change.\n- Capture what should become a dashboard, alert, runbook, or automation.\nCommands:\n$ kubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\n$ kubectl describe pod POD_NAME -n NAMESPACE\n$ kubectl logs deploy/APP -n NAMESPACE --since=15m","audio_url":null,"video_url":null,"course_slug":"platform-networking-fundamentals","course_category":"Networking","course_era":"Platform Academy","vocabulary":[{"id":261,"simplified":"Networking Foundations for Kubernetes","traditional":"Networking Foundations for Kubernetes","pinyin":"Networking","definition":"Build the TCP/IP, DNS, HTTP, TLS, load-balancing, and firewall mental model needed for Kubernetes and AWS troubleshooting."},{"id":262,"simplified":"Evidence","traditional":"Evidence","pinyin":"Debugging","definition":"Observed facts from commands, dashboards, logs, traces, events, or cloud APIs."},{"id":263,"simplified":"Blast radius","traditional":"Blast radius","pinyin":"Reliability","definition":"The set of users, tenants, workloads, or systems affected by a failure or change."},{"id":264,"simplified":"Rollback","traditional":"Rollback","pinyin":"Operations","definition":"A planned path to return to a known-good state when a change causes harm."},{"id":265,"simplified":"Runbook","traditional":"Runbook","pinyin":"Operations","definition":"A step-by-step guide that helps responders diagnose and mitigate an incident safely."}],"flashcards":[{"id":157,"lesson_id":58,"prompt":"What is the main goal of HTTP, TLS, and Ingress?","answer":"Read request symptoms across L7 boundaries.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":158,"lesson_id":58,"prompt":"Why collect evidence before changing live systems?","answer":"It reduces guessing, protects users, and makes the fix reviewable.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":159,"lesson_id":58,"prompt":"What should a learner produce after each lab?","answer":"A clear diagnosis, a safe action plan, and a reusable note for future incidents.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"}]}
